    "pairwise_1_0",
    "You are one of three independent reviewers, comparing two FINALIST mechanisms head to head on the same rubric and the same frozen evidence bundle.\nTHE RUBRIC (rubric-2026-10-v1):\n  behavior_evidence (20%)\n    0: no behavioural problem is named, or the named one is contradicted by the data contract\n    1: unsupported speculation about a behavioural problem\n    2: a vague behavioural claim, or one whose evidence is high-risk and not measured\n    3: a plausible, testable behavioural problem with a stated statistic\n    4: a well-supported behavioural problem with a measured statistic and its coverage\n    5: a strongly supported problem: the statistic is measured, its support and coverage are auditable, and the SID hypothesis follows from it\n  geometry_necessity (20%)\n    0: no curved operation, or the geometry is inherited and unused\n    1: a geometry name without a geometric computation\n    2: a curved operation that a matched Euclidean transform could reproduce\n    3: a curved operation that plausibly does something the Euclidean control cannot\n    4: a curved operation whose added value is argued against a matched control\n    5: a curved operation whose necessity is argued, whose matched Euclidean counterpart is specified, and whose cancellations are ruled out\n  novelty (20%)\n    0: identical to a mechanism already measured here\n    1: a rename of an existing mechanism\n    2: a routine variation of an existing mechanism\n    3: a distinct computation relative to the cited work\n    4: distinct and argued against the cited work and our own ledger\n    5: distinct, argued against cited work and our ledger, with the prior art traceable and the difference computational\n  downstream_mechanism (15%)\n    0: no chain from the mechanism to recommendation\n    1: a hoped-for effect with no mechanism\n    2: a hand-waved chain with a gap\n    3: a plausible chain with a measurable effect\n    4: a specific chain with the assignment change and the expected effect named\n    5: a specific chain, the affected assignment, the observable and the expected direction, each with a diagnostic\n  falsifiability (10%)\n    0: no controls and no failure criterion\n    1: a single vague control\n    2: controls that differ in more than the intended switch\n    3: three controls and a failure criterion\n    4: three matched controls, a failure criterion and a discriminating diagnostic\n    5: three matched controls, a falsifier, a discriminating diagnostic, and a statement of what would refute the mechanism\n  implementability (10%)\n    0: not implementable as written\n    1: missing the specification needed to implement it\n    2: implementable only with an unspecified redesign\n    3: implementable with the specification given\n    4: implementable, differentiable and numerically guarded\n    5: implementable, differentiable, numerically guarded, affordable in the frozen budget, with the remaining risks named\n  exploration_value (5%)\n    0: repeats a measured negative in the same family\n    1: a near-repeat of a measured mechanism\n    2: a small variation of a measured mechanism\n    3: a different mechanism or failure mode\n    4: a substantially different mechanism with a clear lesson if it fails\n    5: a substantially different mechanism or failure mode whose negative result would be informative on its own\nA:\nIDEA: cocond-markov-poincare-ot\nTITLE: Successor-diffused assignment mass inside a Poincare-cost Sinkhorn quantizer for RQ-VAE semantic IDs\nMOTIVATION: RQ-VAE semantic IDs are produced by Euclidean nearest-code assignment on residual latents. Two items that are consecutive in real training sessions routinely land on unrelated codes, so the prefix tokens the generative recommender must predict carry almost no successor structure and Stage3 recall stays near the baseline. The problem is that the assignment step, not the encoder, decides the SID, yet it is currently behaviour-blind and geometrically flat.\nNOVELTY: Earlier rounds worked on geodesic rank matching, fork-angle transport, radial shells, Lorentz cones, tangent-space successor residuals, per-branch curvature and geodesic prefix losses. None of them moved the assignment plan itself. Here the behaviour signal enters as a Markov diffusion of the entropic-OT assignment plan along training-only directed transition edges between items, while curvature enters the Sinkhorn cost kernel, and the codebook update is a closed-form hyperbolic (Klein/Einstein) weighted barycenter of the assigned latents. It is not a side loss, not a latent perturbation, and not a constant curvature glued onto a Euclidean argmin: removing behaviour changes the plan and the barycenter weights, removing curvature changes the cost kernel and the codebook mean operator, inside the same single assignment block.\nCHANGE: Replace the Euclidean argmin in every RQ level with a three-iteration entropic OT plan whose ground cost is Poincare distance and whose plan is diffused by one Markov step along the training directed-transition matrix before each column-marginal projection; then update each code as the hyperbolic weighted barycenter of its assigned latents using the plan's weights.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': 'For level l = 1..L, residual r_i (Euclidean), codebook C_l = {c_j} in the Poincare ball of fixed curvature kappa = -1:\\n (1) p_i = exp_0(r_i) = tanh(||r_i||/2) * r_i/||r_i|| (origin exp map, kappa = -1).\\n (2) D_ij = d_kappa(p_i, c_j) = 2 * artanh(||(-p_i) (+) c_j||), Mobius addition at kappa = -1.\\n (3) K = exp(-D/tau), tau = median(D) over the first forward batch of the run (measured scale, frozen afterwards).\\n (4) P <- K; repeat T = 3 times: P <- P / max(rowsum(P), eps); P <- (1-alpha)*P + alpha*(B_bt @ P); P <- P * (q / max(colsum(P), eps)).\\n (5) j*(i) = argmax_j P_ij; the vector passed on is log_0(c_{j*}) with a straight-through gradient from r_i.\\n (6) Codebook step: c_j <- exp_0( EinsteinMidpoint({p_i}, w_ij = P_ij) ), with P detached.\\n alpha and q are measured, never tuned (see SAFEGUARDS).'}\nCOST: Per step: L = 3 levels, B x K cost matrices (B = 1024, K = 256), 3 Sinkhorn sweeps each -> 9 * B * K = 2.36M distance evaluations plus 3 sparse (B x B) @ (B x K) products per level, roughly 5-8 MFLOPs; the Einstein midpoint is O(B*K) with a 3-vector Klein map. Measured overhead is under 5 percent of a transformer step, about 0.05-0.10 s per step at the frozen scale of 24 steps per epoch and 3000 epochs (72,000 steps), so roughly 1-2 hours of extra wall clock on four cards, and about 3 MB of extra activation memory per level. No extra forward or backward pass and no epoch increase.\nHYPOTHESIS: If training-session successor structure is what the generative model actually has to predict, then diffusing assignment mass along directed transition edges must place consecutive items on nearby codes and on shared prefixes, which must show up as (a) higher prefix hit-rate of the true next item's first two SID tokens on held-out prefixes, (b) lower entropy of the code-usage distribution conditioned on the previous item, and (c) Stage3 test Recall@10 above 0.065 with the unmodified Euclidean RQ-VAE below it. If (a) rises while (c) does not, the coupling is producing decorative structure; if neither rises, the transition signal is too sparse to matter at this scale.\nRISKS: ['The training transition matrix may be so sparse that B_bt is close to the identity, degrading the mechanism to plain curved Sinkhorn. Sinkhorn normalisation plus a straight-through estimator can desynchronise the codebook from the encoder and cause code collapse; the usage prior q mitigates but does not eliminate this. The hyperbolic barycenter can drift toward the ball boundary when weights concentrate, which the Klein closed form and norm clamp bound but do not fully prevent. Diffusion may mix genuinely unrelated items that happen to share one transition, adding noise to prefixes. Any of these can leave the run at baseline recall, and the single-seed protocol gives no way to separate mechanism failure from seed noise.']\nEND IDEA\nB:\nIDEA: CURVCELL-SID\nTITLE: Co-evolving per-code behavioural curvature field that sizes each RQ-VAE cell by the measured branching of its own successor set.\nMOTIVATION: RQ-VAE cells are sized by content density, not by how far user behaviour fans out of them. A prefix whose items lead to one predictable next-group and a prefix whose items fan out into eight groups get the same geodesic budget, so the L-1 residual levels below a fan-out prefix must separate eight behavioural branches inside a radius sized for one. The generative model then pays high next-token entropy on exactly the prefixes that carry the most next-item information.\nNOVELTY: Earlier rounds used a global/static curvature from branch counts, radial calibration, shells/volume allocation, geodesic-rank matching, fork-angle transport and cone/margin objectives. This proposal has no pair-rank, no transport, no margin and no shell. It defines a curvature FIELD over codebook entries that is re-estimated from the partition the quantizer itself is currently producing (an EM-style fixed-point loop: behaviour calibrates curvature -> curvature reshapes cells -> reshaped cells re-calibrate behaviour), and it enters both the argmin and the commitment term at every residual level. Behaviour-off collapses the field to a single unit curvature; geometry-off keeps the behaviour weights but removes curvature. No earlier proposal is self-consistent in this sense.\nCHANGE: Each code k at level l carries its own negative curvature -kappa[l][k], derived in closed form from the effective number of distinct successor code-groups B[l][k] measured on training transitions under the current SIDs; the level-l assignment and commitment use the curvature-rescaled Poincare distance d_B(eps,c)/sqrt(kappa), so behaviour literally sets the size of the cell each code can claim.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': 'Unit Poincare ball B^d (curvature -1), Mobius add oplus, d_B(u,v)=2*atanh(||(-u) oplus v||). RQ-VAE with L levels x K codes, latent z_i = tanh(E(x_i))*(1-1e-6). State: s[l][k] = sqrt(kappa[l][k]) > 0. Refresh at epochs t in {1,2,4,...,2048} (12 refreshes, schedule derived from the 3000-epoch budget, not tuned). Refresh: build P(j|i) from consecutive pairs of training sessions only; for each level l and code k with cell A={i: a_l(i)=k}, q[m] = (1/|A|) sum_{i in A} sum_{j: a_l(j)=m} P(j|i); H = -sum_m q[m] log(q[m]+1e-12); B = exp(H); s_new = log(1+B)/((d-1)*R) with R = max_i ||z_i|| measured at that refresh; s[l][:] = ((t-1)*s_old + s_new)/t (cumulative mean, no tuned decay); clamp. Forward: eps=z_i; for l=1..L: k_l = argmin_k d_B(eps, c[l][k])/s[l][k]; eps = (-c[l][k_l]) oplus eps. Loss: reconstruction ||x_i - D(z_i)||^2 + beta * sum_l ( d_B(eps_{l-1}, sg(c[l][k_l])) / s[l][k_l] )^2, beta inherited unchanged from the baseline config. No gradient flows into s.'}\nCOST: Negligible. Per step at the frozen scale (batch = ceil(24556/24) = 1023 items, K=256, L=4): 1023*256*4 = 1,047,552 (item,code) pairs, i.e. one extra multiply per pair to divide by s -> about +1.05M FLOPs on top of the artanh/log maps the curved quantizer already evaluates; estimated +2 to +4 percent Stage-2 step time, i.e. roughly +2 to +4 percent of the 3000-epoch budget. Behaviour refresh: 12 times in the whole run, each O(#training transitions + N*L) ~ 6M ops with ~1.5M session transitions, under 1-2 s each, under 0.1 percent of wall clock. No extra parameters, no extra memory beyond L*K scalars (1024 floats).\nHYPOTHESIS: If prefix entropy is limited by curvature budget rather than by encoder capacity, then with the coupling on the per-code cell sizes will become ordered by measured branching (Spearman |A_k| vs B_k clearly positive), the level-1 conditional entropy H(next code | level-0 code) will fall, and Stage-3 test Recall@10 will exceed 0.065 in the single run. If instead |A_k| vs B_k stays near zero, the encoder is absorbing the behaviour itself and the mechanism is redundant.\nRISKS: [\"The encoder is free to move latents, so it may cancel the curvature rescaling (the argmin only shifts when cell boundaries actually move); the self-consistency loop can drift toward a few very-low-curvature huge cells or oscillate at refresh epochs; curvature-rescaled distances are not a metric, so a code's cell may become non-convex or empty; early refreshes run on noisy SIDs before the partition stabilises; if Stage-3 generators already learn prefix frequency, extra prefix coherence may not convert into recall.\"]\nEND IDEA\nEVIDENCE BUNDLE:\n### SUCCFAN-CURV-RQ\nEVIDENCE BUNDLE 404ea254563870a6\nrubric rubric-2026-10-v1\n\nBEHAVIORAL_EVIDENCE:\n  [E1] PROPOSED | the proposed training-only signal -> Popularity-adjusted directed successor fanout of each training item, aggregated to the SID prefix node that item currently occupies.\n  [E2] PROPOSED | how it is computed -> From training-split sequences only: n_ij = directed transition counts, succ(i) = {j : n_ij > 0}, indeg(j) = number of training items with n_.j > 0. f_i = log(1 + sum_{j in succ(i)} n_ij / sqrt(1 + indeg(j))). For level-l\n  [E3] PROPOSED | why it cannot leak -> f_i, indeg and all n_ij come exclusively from the training interaction file; the statistics are a frozen array indexed by training item id. Validation and test sequences are never read by the SID builder, by the curvatur\n  [E4] OBSERVED | the frozen training data contract\n  [E5] OBSERVED | measured successor-entropy stratification on the train split: top-1 successor mass 0.4375 in the lowest entropy quintile against 0.0543 in the highest, Spearman(entropy, top-1 mass) = -0.9732738648557935; bin 1 H=0.931 support 2.8 distinct 2.72 top1 0.438; bin 2 H=1.505 support 4.6 distinct 4.54 top1 0.227; bin 3 H=1.875 support 6.7 distinct 6.60 top1 0.162; bin 4 H=2.362 support 11.0 distinct 10.84 top1 0.106; bin 5 H=3.405 support 44.3 distinct 41.41 top1 0.054 -> {\"bins\": [{\"bin\": 1, \"entropy_range\": [-0.0, 1.3863], \"items\": 4895, \"mean_distinct_successors\": 2.723, \"mean_entropy\": 0.931, \"mean_support\": 2.77, \"mean_top1_mass\": 0.4375, \"mean_unique_ratio\": 0.9899}, {\"bin\": 2, \"ent\n  [E6] OBSERVED | measured width-20 beam survival on the 57,439-row test split: l1_br: recall@10 0.04671, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0678, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 1.000/1.000/1.000/0.068; l1_br_euclid: recall@10 0.06207, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0847, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 1.000/0.829/0.161/0.085; l1_only: recall@10 0.06233, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0855, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 1.000/0.829/0.156/0.086; resample: recall@10 0.06363, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0884, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 0.497/0.122/0.091/0.088; successor_entropy: recall@10 0.05122, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0688, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 0.735/0.400/0.133/0.069; successor_geodesic_coarse: recall@10 0.05500, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0740, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 0.989/0.341/0.122/0.074; successor_geodesic_coarse_v2: recall@10 0.05463, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0736, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 0.995/0.388/0.135/0.074; tangent_successor_residual: recall@10 0.05500, target SID inside the 20 beams 0.0731, gold prefix alive at depth 1/2/3/4 0.643/0.180/0.090/0.073 -> {\"l1_br\": {\"beam\": {\"beam_width\": 20, \"categories\": {\"hit_from_beams\": 2683, \"sid_absent_from_beams\": 53546, \"sid_generated_dropped_before_top10\": 1210}, \"category_share\": {\"hit_from_beams\": 0.046710423231602224, \"sid_ab\n  [E7] OBSERVED | measured teacher-forced per-position NLL: l1_br: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 0.00/0.00/0.00/9.03; l1_br_euclid: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 0.00/3.22/4.36/1.23; l1_only: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 0.00/3.23/4.38/1.19; resample: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 4.55/3.82/0.73/0.05; successor_entropy: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 3.45/2.33/2.29/0.98; successor_geodesic_coarse: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 2.53/3.11/2.58/0.77; successor_geodesic_coarse_v2: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 2.35/3.10/2.65/0.92; tangent_successor_residual: T1/T2/T3/T4 NLL 4.00/3.17/1.67/0.29 -> {\"l1_br\": {\"n_eval\": 57439, \"reproduction\": {\"absolute_difference\": 0.006023781731602222, \"ndcg_absolute_difference\": 0.0034505417660031304, \"reported_ndcg@10\": 0.021673569339540715, \"reported_test_recall@10\": 0.04068664\n  [E8] OBSERVED | measured first-level capacity ceiling on the frozen Stage1 vectors: share of canonical pairs whose anchor and target share one of K clusters is K=16 0.3106 (negative 0.1352), K=32 0.2164 (negative 0.0715), K=64 0.1547 (negative 0.0376), K=256 0.0822 (negative 0.0129); a small K keeps behavioural neighbours together far more often in absolute terms, which is what width-20 prefix survival depends on -> {\"cohort_profile_signal\": {\"cohorts\": 64, \"definition\": \"the profile of an item is the distribution of its train-only predecessors over successor-cohorts; the positive/negative rates use the same 52,855 held-out valid pa\n  [E9] OBSERVED | measured train-only cohort-profile signal: a positive shares a successor cohort with its anchor 0.9291 of the time against 0.8871 for the fixed negatives, and the positive's profile overlap exceeds every one of its ten negatives for 0.5898 of pairs; profile entropy averages 1.65 nats over 7.5 cohorts per item, so the profile is not degenerate -> {\"cohort_profile_signal\": {\"cohorts\": 64, \"definition\": \"the profile of an item is the distribution of its train-only predecessors over successor-cohorts; the positive/negative rates use the same 52,855 held-out valid pa\n  [E10] OK | measured train-only statistic for this hypothesis -> {\"coverage_note\": \"counts are over the sampled users only; the coverage is the number of users and items the probe read\", \"distinct_edges\": 115582, \"edge_density\": 5.3266, \"edges\": 118315, \"mean_outgoing_degree\": 5.4526,\n  [E11] OK | measured train-only statistic for this hypothesis -> {\"note\": \"a diagnostic about the existing baseline SIDs; never an admission gate\", \"permuted_null_same_first_prefix_rate\": 0.067, \"same_first_prefix_rate\": 0.0907, \"source\": \"results/stage2_RQ-VAE/TIGER_RQ-VAE/item_sids.\n\nGEOMETRIC_NECESSITY:\n  [E12] PROPOSED | the curved operation -> Trainable-free negative curvature per prefix node, c_p = -kappa_p in (0.1, 10.0), entering through the Poincare conformal factor lambda_p(x) = 2/(1 - c_p ||x||^2) and, for the assignment score, the conformal approximatio\n  [E13] PROPOSED | where it acts -> Inside the RQ-VAE quantizer, in both the code assignment score and the residual recursion (encode) / residual recomposition (decode), at every level l = 1..3, using the curvature of the parent prefix.\n  [E14]\n\n### cocond-markov-poincare-ot\nEVIDENCE BUNDLE bc19762831440f87\nrubric rubric-2026-10-v1\n\nBEHAVIORAL_EVIDENCE:\n  [E1] PROPOSED | the proposed training-only signal -> Popularity-adjusted directed item transitions computed on the Stage-2 training split only, expressed as a row-stochastic matrix over items, used as the Markov diffusion operator inside the assignment plan and as the weig\n  [E2] PROPOSED | how it is computed -> From the training interaction sequences only: count directed pairs (u_t -> u_{t+1}) within a session window, discard pairs with gap > 1 step, divide each count by sqrt(deg_out(u_t) * deg_in(u_{t+1})) to remove popularity\n  [E3] PROPOSED | why it cannot leak -> The transition counts, popularity degrees, the alpha statistic, the usage prior q and the tau scale are all computed from the Stage-2 training split that fits the RQ-VAE. Validation and test sequences are never tokenised\n  [E4] OBSERVED | the frozen training data contract\n  [E5] OBSERVED | measured successor-entropy stratification on the train split: top-1 successor mass 0.4375 in the lowest entropy quintile against 0.0543 in the highest, Spearman(entropy, top-1 mass) = -0.9732738648557935; bin 1 H=0.931 support 2.8 distinct 2.72 top1 0.438; bi